<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!-- The courier: hector's quadrotor with a laser on a mast above it and a
     grasp pad hung underneath.

     Neither half is new - the mast comes from uav_bushfire, the pad from
     uav_pick_place - but putting both on one airframe is, and it is worth
     saying why that is safe, because the two packages it comes from each warn
     at length about payload inertia.

     The controller reads mass and moment of inertia from Gazebo exactly once,
     when it loads (quadrotor_hardware_gazebo.cpp, getMassAndInertia), and is
     never told anything afterwards. The important distinction is therefore
     between mass that is present at load time and mass that arrives later:

       * The mast and its Hokuyo are in this URDF. Gazebo folds fixed-joint
         children into base_link, so the lumped inertia the controller reads at
         startup already includes them. The laser adds 0.27 kg at +0.25 m,
         which is 0.0169 kg.m^2 about roll and pitch against the bare
         airframe's 0.01152 - a 147% increase, and completely harmless, because
         the controller multiplies its attitude PID output by the inertia it
         read. Bigger inertia, proportionally bigger torque, same response.

       * A box welded on at runtime is not in this URDF and never reaches the
         controller. That inertia is invisible, so it is the only one that has
         to be small. At 0.04 kg held 0.26 m below base_link it is 0.0027,
         about 23% of the bare airframe - the figure uav_pick_place settled on
         after a 0.15 kg box on a longer stem produced a sustained +-3 degree
         roll limit cycle that never decayed and timed out every waypoint.

     So the heavy thing above the aircraft is fine and the light thing below it
     is the constraint, which is the opposite way round from what the masses
     suggest. Raise the box mass in worlds/depot_yard.world and this stops
     flying long before the thrust budget notices: force/z caps at 30 N against
     a 14.5 N airframe, so there is about 1.5 kg of lift spare and none of it
     is usable.

     The mast is 0.25 m because a quadrotor pitches in order to translate.
     Nothing on the airframe is above z = +0.04, so a shorter mast is clear
     while hovering level and useless in flight: the rotor tips are 0.396 m
     out, so 15 degrees of pitch swings the far side of the scan plane down
     0.396*sin(15) = 0.10 m, straight through the rotor disc. Measured at
     0.12 m, exactly that happened - the drone saw its own booms at 0.40 m the
     moment it accelerated and decided it was walled in. 0.25 m keeps the plane
     clear to about 32 degrees, which is where the courier's self_hit_tilt
     comes from.

     Rays are cut from hector's 1081 to 181 and the update rate from 40 Hz to
     10 Hz: the courier only asks this scan for the minimum range in three wide
     sectors, which 181 rays over 270 degrees resolves to about 1.5 degrees. -->
<robot name="quadrotor" xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <xacro:property name="M_PI" value="3.1415926535897931" />

  <!-- How far the grasp plane sits below base_link. Keep in step with
       ~grasp_reach in launch/one_uav.launch: the courier flies to
       (box top + grasp_reach) to put the pad on a box, and every vertical
       number in the pickup and delivery descents is derived from it. -->
  <xacro:property name="grasp_reach" value="0.21" />

  <xacro:include filename="$(find hector_quadrotor_description)/urdf/quadrotor_base.urdf.xacro" />
  <xacro:quadrotor_base_macro />

  <material name="courier_orange">
    <color rgba="0.95 0.45 0.05 1.0" />
  </material>
  <material name="courier_grey">
    <color rgba="0.25 0.25 0.28 1.0" />
  </material>

  <!-- ================================ mast ================================
       Cosmetic - it gives the scanner something to visibly stand on - but a
       real link with a real (tiny) mass, so the inertia the controller reads
       at startup includes it. -->
  <link name="laser_mast">
    <inertial>
      <mass value="0.01" />
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" />
      <inertia ixx="1e-6" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="1e-6" iyz="0" izz="1e-6" />
    </inertial>
    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <geometry>
        <cylinder radius="0.007" length="0.23" />
      </geometry>
      <material name="courier_grey" />
    </visual>
  </link>

  <joint name="laser_mast_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link" />
    <child  link="laser_mast" />
    <origin xyz="0 0 0.135" rpy="0 0 0" />
  </joint>

  <xacro:include filename="$(find hector_sensors_description)/urdf/hokuyo_utm30lx.urdf.xacro" />
  <xacro:hokuyo_utm30lx name="laser0" parent="base_link" ros_topic="scan"
                        update_rate="10" ray_count="181"
                        min_angle="-135" max_angle="135">
    <origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.25" rpy="0 0 0" />
  </xacro:hokuyo_utm30lx>

  <!-- =============================== gripper ==============================
       A pad and a stem below the landing gear, marking where the drone picks
       things up. Visual and inertial only, deliberately NO collision:
       grasping here is a magnet rather than a hand - link_attacher welds the
       box on with a fixed joint, so nothing is squeezed and no contact is
       needed. A collision box would only give the descent a way to knock the
       box out from under the aircraft before the weld lands.

       0.21 m is set by the landing gear. The feet are the lowest part of the
       airframe, a ring between z = -0.19 and z = -0.17 at radius 0.125-0.144.
       A 0.10 m box held with its top face on the pad hangs from -0.21 to
       -0.31, entirely below the feet with 0.02 m to spare. Lengthen the stem
       and the drone flies worse for the m*d^2 reason above; shorten it past
       0.19 and the aircraft stands on the box it is trying to collect. -->
  <joint name="gripper_joint" type="fixed">
    <parent link="base_link" />
    <child  link="gripper_link" />
    <origin xyz="0 0 ${-grasp_reach}" rpy="0 0 0" />
  </joint>

  <!-- gripper_link's origin IS the grasp plane, so both visuals sit at
       positive z from it: the pad just above the origin, the stem above that,
       reaching back up to the underside of the airframe at -0.05. -->
  <link name="gripper_link">
    <inertial>
      <mass value="0.02" />
      <origin xyz="0 0 0.05" />
      <inertia ixx="1e-5" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyy="1e-5" iyz="0.0" izz="1e-5" />
    </inertial>

    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0.01" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <geometry>
        <box size="0.09 0.09 0.02" />
      </geometry>
      <material name="courier_orange" />
    </visual>

    <visual>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0.09" rpy="0 0 0" />
      <geometry>
        <cylinder radius="0.008" length="0.14" />
      </geometry>
      <material name="courier_grey" />
    </visual>
  </link>

  <!-- Gazebo folds a fixed joint's child into its parent, so in the simulator
       gripper_link's visuals become part of base_link and there is no separate
       body by that name. That is what we want - a rigid pad, not a flapping
       one - and it is why the courier welds boxes to base_link rather than to
       gripper_link, which Gazebo would not be able to find. The link still
       exists in the URDF, so robot_state_publisher puts a gripper_link frame
       on TF where the pad actually is. -->

  <xacro:include filename="$(find hector_quadrotor_gazebo)/urdf/quadrotor_plugins.gazebo.xacro" />

</robot>
